<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf8">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="./spec.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/highlight.js/8.4/styles/github.min.css">
<script src="./spec.js"></script>
<pre class="metadata">
title: Compartments
stage: 1
contributors: Mark S. Miller, Caridy Patiño, Patrick Soquet, Kris Kowal, Jack Works, Guy Bedford
</pre>

<emu-intro id="sec-intro">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>
    A Compartment is an object that owns a mapping from module source keys to
    module instances, evaluated against a chosen global object. This proposal
    specifies a minimal Compartment surface that composes with the rest of module
    harmony by taking the intersection of the related proposals rather than
    layering a parallel module system beside them.
  </p>
  <p>
    This document is a scaffold. Each clause below names a part of the
    intersection surface and will be filled in as the design settles. Prior
    iterations of the proposal are preserved in the repository's `archive/`
    directory and are not normative here.
  </p>
  <emu-note>
    <p>
      Two commitments shape this specification. First, a ModuleSource is treated
      as an opaque key for indexing a module instance within a Compartment; the
      SES-era module descriptor concept is not used. Second, a Compartment must be
      able to evaluate modules against the surrounding realm's global object, so
      that the design is viable for hosts such as Node.js that do not want to pay
      for an additional global runtime context.
    </p>
  </emu-note>
</emu-intro>

<emu-clause id="sec-module-source-key">
  <h1>ModuleSource as an Opaque Key</h1>
  <p>
    A ModuleSource produced by the source phase of a module is used as an opaque
    key. A Compartment holds at most one module instance per ModuleSource key.
    This clause will define the identity and lifetime rules for that key.
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-compartment-objects">
  <h1>Compartment Objects</h1>
  <p>This clause will define the Compartment abstraction and its instances.</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-constructor">
    <h1>The Compartment Constructor</h1>
    <p>This clause will define construction, including the choice of global object.</p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-prototype-import">
    <h1>Compartment.prototype.import</h1>
    <p>This clause will define asynchronous module loading and evaluation.</p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-module-indexing">
    <h1>Indexing Module Instances by ModuleSource Key</h1>
    <p>This clause will define the per-Compartment source-key to instance mapping.</p>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-global-object-sharing">
  <h1>Sharing the Surrounding Realm's Global Object</h1>
  <p>
    This clause will specify how a Compartment evaluates modules against the
    surrounding realm's global object, so that no additional global runtime
    context is required. This is the binding constraint for Node.js viability.
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-intersection-source-phase-imports">
  <h1>Intersection with Source Phase Imports</h1>
  <p>
    This clause will specify how a Compartment's source keys relate to the static
    source phase import form (import source ... from ...).
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-intersection-source-phase-import-expression">
  <h1>Intersection with the Source Phase Import Expression</h1>
  <p>
    This clause will specify how a Compartment relates to the source phase import
    expression (the dynamic source phase import form).
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-intersection-import-defer">
  <h1>Intersection with Import Defer</h1>
  <p>
    This clause will specify how deferred module evaluation composes with a
    Compartment's source-key indexing.
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-loader-hooks">
  <h1>Loading and Resolution</h1>
  <p>
    This clause will specify the intersection with module virtualization: how a
    Compartment resolves specifiers and obtains ModuleSource values.
  </p>
</emu-clause>
